hey this is sarah with  registerednessrn.com and today i'm going  to show you how to start an iv in my  previous videos i've showed you how to  prepare your iv kit and how to find a  vein on a patient and a different size  of iv gauge needles so if you haven't  watched those you probably want to watch  those videos before you actually watch  this um so today we have miss smith and  we're gonna she's our mannequin and  we're gonna start an iv on her  before you ever start an iv you want to  get your supplies um here's what i have  for my supplies we have a 22 ivy gauge  needle  and extension tubing that will connect  to the cannula of the iv you can use a  cap it's whatever your preference is  a saline flush to prime the line and to  ensure that our iv is patent once we get  it and an iv start kit that contains our  tourniquet or cleaning supplies or tape  everything and of course a pair of  gloves  after you gather your supplies  go to your patient and explain to them  what you'll be doing explain what the iv  will be used for  how it's inserted the pain they're going  to feel a little bit of pain and  just talk to them and share let them  share their concerns with you because a  lot of patients they hate starting an iv  where i work um we get outpatients and  the very first thing we have to do is  start an iv and they say if i knew that  i was getting an iv i probably wouldn't  even came for the test so people really  hate ivs  so what i'm going to do first is i'm  going to get my tubing set up and i'm  going to put the tourniquet on the  patient and look for a vein  so your kit comes with your tourniquet  i have a video on how to put a  tourniquet on a patient before you start  an iv or draw blood there's a trick to  that so what you want to do is i like to  put my tourniquet  above the bend of the arm and  just do tie that with a quick release  and i like to have the patient  sort of put their arm down  so gravity will have the blood pulling  the extremities  and have the patient pump the fist  miss smith can't find purpose because  she's a mannequin but in a real person  when that happens the veins will engorge  and you'll be able to find them  typically where i like to look i like to  look in the ac region or the hand and  people like to do the forearm it's  really your preference what you've got  good at every year over the years so  here's the ac area and in the hands i  like miss smith's hand vane so we're  going to go with that  and while that vein is getting nice and  engorged that's going to help you  whenever a vein is nice and puffy that  decreases your chances of missing the  vein because a lot of times the veins  like to go flat and really just miss  them and you have to try again  so while our veins getting nice and  engorged we're going to get our hip  prepped  and get her extension tubing blush so  i've done hand hygiene so i'm going to  put my gloves on  and always be careful while you're  messing with your supplies because you  don't want to contaminate it because  this is going in the bloodstream  and you don't want it to introduce any  germs into the blood  so like what i like to do first is i  like to prime my extension tubing or my  cap just to get the air out of the  extension tubing so i'm going to get the  tubing out  and get my saline flush  and these are pre-filled flushes i love  it because i remember a long time ago  you had to draw up your own saline so i  love pre-filled saline plushies saline  flush has a bubble in it you have to  prime it and get the bubble out you  don't want to inject that air in the  patient so what you do is you just take  the plunger and you just push it up and  have a little bit saline come out you're  going to screw this onto your extension  tubing extension tubings vary by  hospital settings  and so what i'm going to do is i'm just  going to screw that in on the side  and i'm going to flush my tubing just to  get any air out and it will leak a  little bit that's good that means that  you're flushing the tubing i'm going to  keep my saline screwed onto this  extension tubing and this cap on just to  keep it  clean because whenever you actually  start your ivy you don't want to be  fumbling trying to screw your saline on  while you're holding an iv down to  prevent blood from coming out so i just  take that and i just put that back into  the  little packaging  next what i like to do is i like to get  my tape ready  so take your tape out  and i peel  i don't know about four to five inches  off  and i like to  tear it in half because i use these two  pieces to secure the iv line  in place people do it differently this  is just how i do it  i like to just set that aside  because i know i'll be using that  next and gloves and tape do not go  together  there we go  next i'm going to clean the site  in your kit um this kit  came with betadine and alcohol prep a  lot of the other kids are coming with  chlorhexidine it's like a really strong  alcohol that kills germs but this kit  did not come with it if your kit does  come with betadine make sure your  patient isn't allergic  so i'm going to take this alcohol and  i'm going to clean the side i'm going to  clean it  about 10 to 15 seconds in a circular  motion  really killing any germs or any normal  flora that's on her skin so i don't want  to introduce that into the bloodstream  whenever i go to insert the iv so we're  getting that nice and clean  and  then you let it completely dry  don't blow on it because when you blow  on it you're gonna put your germs on it  i've seen people do that don't blow on  it just let it  air dry itself  and be getting your iv ready this is a  22 gauge um miss smith is just going to  be having some basic testing sheets so  she doesn't need an 18 gauge or a 20  gauge she won't be getting blood so  we're just going to do a basic 22. so  it's always good to test what size iv  you need to start on your patient  because patients do not like to be stuck  with an iv and then you come and tell  them oh the doctors ordered a ct with p  protocol you need an 18 gauge so always  try to do the right gauge needle  so what we're going to do i like on  these plastic cannulas again your  hospital will vary on what they have but  i like to just move that around because  sometimes there's like this little glue  on it and when you go to advance it  won't advance so just sort of break that  seal a little bit  okay  so we're gonna start our iv  so we have our vein there  it's good for the patient to make a fist  sometimes prevent those hand veins from  rolling because handmades like to roll  so  have your patient make a fist if it is a  roller and i see the vein right there so  i'm just going to go in  okay  now once you go in you ask yourself how  do i know i'm in the vein  look in this little chamber right here  in this chamber you will see blood start  to collect that is a great sign that  means that you have hit the bank  sometimes you can see the blood come up  through the cannula  and that means you hit the vein  so once that happens what you need to do  is just advance just a little bit more  just a little  push because i have noticed i hit the  vein when i hit the vein i try to  advance the cannula like that  and it won't advance even though i'm in  the vein typically what you do if you  advance it puts the needle a little bit  better in the vein because normally the  needle is just right  barely in the vein when you try to put  the candle in it doesn't go so just  insert just a little bit more  and then slide your cannula in you can  use this  tip right here to slide it in  and this plastic candle is sliding off  of your needle and it's going in the  vein  and your needle is going to stay with  you now after you do that see this  button that is a safety that's to  protect you hit this  and your needle goes straight into this  chamber protects you from any sticks and  you put this in your sharp spots  okay we have that normally blood will  start gushing out so you need to just  hold above where the candle is like that  with your thumb and it'll prevent the  blood from coming out i remember when i  was first starting ivs i would have a  huge mess because i wouldn't do that so  always put your hand right there and  while you're doing that go over to  your extension tubing  and  take the cap off that and just screw  into this part right here  always screw this  very snugly because a lot of times i've  been giving medications because i work  in a testing area and the iv wasn't  screwed on very well and um we have to  give radioactive isotope and from where  the person who started the ivy didn't  screw it on well it would leak so always  make sure you have that screwed on well  and we're going to flush with the saline  flush  in the iv and what you're looking for is  you're looking for it to puff up around  the side if it starts to puff up that's  a bad sign that means that your iv is  infiltrated and the saline is going in  the tissues and not in the vein take it  out and do a new iv  or if it's burning the patient because  sometimes the iv will be infiltrated and  you won't see it puffing up  and it'll be infiltrated even though you  don't see it because the patient's  telling you oh that's burning really bad  because the saline's in there think  about when you get salt in your eyes it  burns so if saline gets  in the tissues it's going to burn now  it's normal for the patient to say oh it  feels like something's dripping down my  arm or it's cold or i taste it or i  smell it that's a great slime that means  it's in the vein so we flushed it and  it's in there so what we're going to do  is we're just going to take the saline  off  and we're going to tape our iv down  that is another important part you  always want to take your iv down good  because i remember one time i worked  really hard and getting my iv  and i didn't tape it down right and the  patient got up to use the bathroom and  lost their iv and i had to do another  one  so i like to take my tape and  put it underneath  this part the cannula part  and tape and gloves do not go together  i'm just going to tape it down about  right there first so i can secure it  and i'm going to take this other piece  of tape that i tore and  just put it underneath this  and i like to just lace it around it  ah these gloves and tape are so sticky  okay then i am going to put the tegaderm  on it  that  and we'll just tape it down a little bit  more  and always clamp this part off so blood  won't pull in there  and you have to really peel this tape  back  so it'll stick  there we go  and sometimes it's really good to secure  it with some tegaderm  kids didn't come with tiger durham for  some reason so we won't have that but  always put your tegaderm around that and  clamp your thing and put your date and  time on it so the oncoming nurses  or anyone who takes the patient will  know  when you started it what gauge it is and  i know in the computer we can chart it  so always pop your tourniquet off after  you're done  and we're done so that's how you start  an iv on a patient  and  if you have any questions or anything be  sure to visit my website  registerednessonline.com because that's  where um you can find all the articles  you need about nursing skills and talk  about nursing we have a forum where you  can connect with others so thank you so  much for watching if you like this video  please share it with others

